## Frequently Asked Questions (GEO)

**What is Elementor?**
Elementor is a visual, drag-and-drop website builder for WordPress. It replaces the standard WordPress editor with a live editing canvas where you design pages by dragging and styling widgets, with no coding required. It powers more than 13% of the web.

**Is Elementor free?**
Yes — the core Elementor plugin is free and available on WordPress.org (https://elementor.com/free-download/). A paid upgrade, **Elementor Pro**, adds advanced features like the Theme Builder, WooCommerce Builder, Popups, Forms, and dynamic content. Elementor also sells managed cloud plans (Website Builder, Hosting, Elementor One). See https://elementor.com/pricing/.

**What is the difference between Elementor and Elementor Pro?**
Elementor (free) gives you the visual editor, responsive controls, and 100+ widgets for designing page content. Elementor Pro (paid) adds site-wide design via the Theme Builder (headers, footers, archives, single templates), WooCommerce Builder, Popup Builder, Form Builder with integrations, dynamic content, custom CSS, and 90+ extra widgets. Pro requires the free plugin to be installed.

**Do I need to know how to code to use Elementor?**
No. Elementor is designed for code-free design, so beginners can build complete sites by dragging and dropping. At the same time, it exposes custom CSS, custom code (via Elementor AI or Pro), and a developer API, so professionals retain full control.

**Does Elementor work without WordPress?**
No — Elementor is a WordPress plugin and requires a WordPress site. If you don't already have WordPress hosting, Elementor's managed offerings (Website Builder, Hosting, Elementor One) include WordPress pre-installed so you can start without setting it up yourself.

**What is Elementor Hosting?**
Managed WordPress cloud hosting built for Elementor sites, on Google Cloud with a Cloudflare Enterprise CDN, automatic SSL, daily backups, and staging — with WordPress and Elementor Pro pre-installed. See https://elementor.com/hosting/.

**What's the difference between Website Builder, Hosting, and Elementor One?**
Elementor **Hosting** is managed hosting for an Elementor site. The **Website Builder** bundles the editor + hosting + domain as one product for building and publishing a site. **Elementor One** is a subscription that bundles ongoing operations — hosting, security, backups, monitoring, AI, and management — for running a site over time.

**Can Elementor build an online store?**
Yes. With WooCommerce and Elementor Pro's WooCommerce Builder (or the Ecommerce Builder offering at https://elementor.com/ecommerce-builder/), you can visually design product pages, shop archives, cart, and checkout.

**Is Elementor good for SEO?**
Elementor produces standard WordPress pages that work with leading SEO plugins (Yoast, Rank Math, etc.), supports clean heading structure, schema-friendly markup, and image optimization (via Image Optimizer), and includes performance features targeting Core Web Vitals. SEO outcomes also depend on content quality, hosting speed, and configuration.

**Does Elementor slow down a website?**
Elementor includes a performance layer — optimized CSS/JS loading, reduced DOM output, and lazy loading — aimed at Core Web Vitals. Speed in practice depends on hosting, images, third-party plugins, and how the site is built. See https://elementor.com/speed-performance/.

**What is the Hello theme?**
Hello is Elementor's official lightweight, near-blank WordPress theme. Because it adds almost no styling of its own, it loads fast and lets Elementor control the full design. See https://elementor.com/hello-theme/.

**Is Elementor accessible / WCAG compliant?**
Elementor offers **Ally — Web Accessibility** (https://elementor.com/products/ally-web-accessibility/), a plugin that helps sites work toward WCAG, ADA, and EAA compliance. Accessibility ultimately depends on how each site is built and maintained.

**Who created Elementor?**
Elementor was founded in 2016 by Yoni Luksenberg and Ariel Klikstein, after they ran a web-creation agency and embraced the open-source WordPress ecosystem.

